    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Maybe you’ve seen this in a sci-fi film: several, or even hundreds, of bug-sized drones working together. For years, scientists have tried to turn this scene into a reality."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "However, there’s a problem. These machines are too "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b1",
            "number": 1
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to have enough processing power. The same "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b2",
            "number": 2
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " also prevents other larger robots from navigating independently. Researchers from the Delft University of Technology (TU Delft) in the Netherlands believe they may have found a solution. It’s inspired by the ant."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Ants, and insects in general, are capable of embarking on long journeys to look for food. It seems that they always "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b3",
            "number": 3
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " how to get back home. Desert ants in the Sahara, for example, can travel more than two kilometres away from their nests without "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b4",
            "number": 4
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "How do they achieve this? Researchers think ants may find their way home by using a “snapshot model”"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "快照模式"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". That is to say, they “take photos” of the "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b5",
            "number": 5
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " around them with their brain. Later, when they are travelling, the insects try to match things around them with the "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b6",
            "number": 6
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in their mind. It’s a very effective way of finding directions."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "TU Delft researchers wanted to see whether they could "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b7",
            "number": 7
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " those insect-world observations for a 56-gram flying drone. The drone was equipped with a tiny camera and a cheap processor. The researchers then created an indoor obstacle course. They also offered several snapshots for the drone to reference."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The drone moved from point to point along the course "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b8",
            "number": 8
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " the snapshots. After that, it returned to its home base. In the end, the drone "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b9",
            "number": 9
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " travelled 100 metres around the course. And it used just 0.65 kilobytes"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "千字节"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of memory."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The snapshot experiments could one day help small drones find their way home on their own. They may "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b10",
            "number": 10
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " need heavy, complex sensors or GPS."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
